Run Python in your terminal on the Jetson:

Bash
python3 -c "from pymongo import MongoClient; client = MongoClient('mongodb+srv://bharati_admin:Cantfakeall@cluster0.pxsvmko.mongodb.net/?appName=Cluster0', serverSelectionTimeoutMS=5000); print(client.server_info())"
If it prints a large dictionary of server info: The Jetson can connect to MongoDB Atlas properly.

If it times out or throws ServerSelectionTimeoutError / ConfigurationError:

Ensure DNS resolution is working for mongodb+srv (install dnspython: pip3 install dnspython).

Check Network Access in Atlas to confirm 0.0.0.0/0 is marked active.

2. Test Local Endpoint on the Jetson
In a separate terminal on the Jetson, test the login endpoint directly over localhost:

Bash
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/login \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"username": "admin", "password": "admin"}'
If this returns {"status":"success", ...}: FastAPI and MongoDB are functioning correctly locally.

If this returns an error or hangs: Inspect the traceback directly in the terminal where uvicorn is running.

3. Test the Zrok Public URL from your Laptop
On your laptop (or via terminal), run:

Bash
curl -X POST https://testbharatibackend.shares.zrok.io/login \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"username": "admin", "password": "admin"}'
If this succeeds: The tunnel and backend are healthy; any remaining browser issue is purely frontend fetch configuration.

If this fails or times out: The Zrok tunnel is not forwarding traffic to port 8000 on the Jetson.